1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,566
November 24, 1971.

2
00:00:01,566 --> 00:00:02,819
Thanksgiving Eve.

3
00:00:02,819 --> 00:00:05,952
At Portland International Airport, a quiet man in a dark

4
00:00:05,952 --> 00:00:09,398
business suit walks to the Northwest Orient Airlines counter.

5
00:00:09,398 --> 00:00:12,844
He pays cash for a one-way ticket to Seattle.

6
00:00:12,844 --> 00:00:15,663
The name he gives is Dan Cooper.

7
00:00:15,663 --> 00:00:17,856
Nothing about him attracts attention.

8
00:00:17,856 --> 00:00:20,582
Witnesses later describe a man in his mid-forties,

9
00:00:20,582 --> 00:00:22,681
wearing a white shirt, black tie,

10
00:00:22,681 --> 00:00:23,934
and suit.

11
00:00:23,934 --> 00:00:26,033
He boards Northwest Orient Flight 305,

12
00:00:26,033 --> 00:00:28,539
a Boeing 727 carrying thirty-six passengers.

13
00:00:28,539 --> 00:00:31,891
He takes a seat near the rear of the cabin,

14
00:00:31,891 --> 00:00:34,710
orders a bourbon and soda, and waits.

15
00:00:34,710 --> 00:00:37,122
A little after three in the afternoon,

16
00:00:37,122 --> 00:00:39,942
Cooper hands a flight attendant a note.

17
00:00:39,942 --> 00:00:43,074
At first she assumes it is a phone number and

18
00:00:43,074 --> 00:00:45,267
slips it into her purse.

19
00:00:45,267 --> 00:00:48,400
Cooper quietly tells her she should read it.

20
00:00:48,400 --> 00:00:51,219
The message says he has a bomb.

21
00:00:51,219 --> 00:00:53,099
She sits beside him.

22
00:00:53,099 --> 00:00:56,232
Cooper opens an attaché case just enough for her to

23
00:00:56,232 --> 00:00:58,424
see wires and red-colored cylinders.

24
00:00:58,424 --> 00:01:01,557
Whether the device is real is never proven.

25
00:01:01,557 --> 00:01:04,063
What matters is what happens next.

26
00:01:04,063 --> 00:01:07,196
Cooper dictates his demands: two hundred thousand dollars in twenty-dollar

27
00:01:07,196 --> 00:01:10,329
bills, four parachutes, and a fuel truck waiting when the

28
00:01:10,329 --> 00:01:12,208
plane lands in Seattle.

29
00:01:12,208 --> 00:01:15,341
The crew contacts authorities while the aircraft circles.

30
00:01:15,341 --> 00:01:18,474
On the ground, the FBI and local officials begin assembling

31
00:01:18,474 --> 00:01:19,727
the ransom.

32
00:01:19,727 --> 00:01:22,859
The serial numbers of the bills are recorded.

33
00:01:22,859 --> 00:01:24,426
Parachutes are obtained.

34
00:01:24,426 --> 00:01:27,151
When Flight 305 finally lands at Seattle-Tacoma Airport,

35
00:01:27,151 --> 00:01:29,971
Cooper gets exactly what he asked for.

36
00:01:29,971 --> 00:01:33,416
He releases all thirty-six passengers and several crew members.

37
00:01:33,416 --> 00:01:36,236
But the story is only halfway over.

38
00:01:36,236 --> 00:01:39,369
Cooper orders the remaining crew to take the aircraft south

39
00:01:39,369 --> 00:01:40,935
toward Mexico City.

40
00:01:40,935 --> 00:01:43,974
He specifies a low altitude, the landing gear down,

41
00:01:43,974 --> 00:01:46,793
the cabin unpressurized, and a slow speed.

42
00:01:46,793 --> 00:01:50,145
Those instructions matter because the Boeing 727 has something unusual:

43
00:01:50,145 --> 00:01:53,591
a rear staircase that can be lowered in flight.

44
00:01:53,591 --> 00:01:55,690
Sometime after eight o'clock that night,

45
00:01:55,690 --> 00:01:59,042
flying through darkness and bad weather over the Pacific Northwest,

46
00:01:59,042 --> 00:02:02,488
the crew feels a sudden change in the aircraft.

47
00:02:02,488 --> 00:02:04,994
The rear staircase has been deployed.

48
00:02:04,994 --> 00:02:07,406
When the plane later lands in Reno,

49
00:02:07,406 --> 00:02:08,972
Cooper is gone.

50
00:02:08,972 --> 00:02:12,732
So are the ransom money and one of the parachutes.

51
00:02:12,732 --> 00:02:15,457
The FBI gives the investigation a code name:

52
00:02:15,457 --> 00:02:17,650
NORJAK, short for Northwest Hijacking.

53
00:02:17,650 --> 00:02:19,749
Agents search the aircraft, interview witnesses,

54
00:02:19,749 --> 00:02:23,195
examine physical evidence, and chase leads across the country.

55
00:02:23,195 --> 00:02:26,327
Over the years, hundreds of people are considered.

56
00:02:26,327 --> 00:02:29,460
By the fifth anniversary, investigators have looked at more than

57
00:02:29,460 --> 00:02:31,026
eight hundred suspects.

58
00:02:31,026 --> 00:02:33,533
But Cooper leaves behind very little.

59
00:02:33,533 --> 00:02:35,412
A black clip-on tie.

60
00:02:35,412 --> 00:02:38,232
Cigarette butts that would later be lost.

61
00:02:38,232 --> 00:02:41,364
A few fingerprints that cannot be conclusively tied to the

62
00:02:41,364 --> 00:02:42,304
hijacker.

63
00:02:42,304 --> 00:02:45,437
And the memories of flight attendants who sat only feet

64
00:02:45,437 --> 00:02:47,003
away from him.

65
00:02:47,003 --> 00:02:49,822
Then, nine years later, the case changes.

66
00:02:49,822 --> 00:02:52,955
In 1980, an eight-year-old boy named Brian Ingram is digging

67
00:02:52,955 --> 00:02:56,088
in the sand along the Columbia River when he finds

68
00:02:56,088 --> 00:02:58,594
three decaying bundles of twenty-dollar bills.

69
00:02:58,594 --> 00:03:01,727
The total is five thousand eight hundred dollars.

70
00:03:01,727 --> 00:03:04,546
The serial numbers match the Cooper ransom.

71
00:03:04,546 --> 00:03:07,679
The discovery creates a new mystery instead of solving the

72
00:03:07,679 --> 00:03:08,932
old one.

73
00:03:08,932 --> 00:03:11,751
How did the money reach the riverbank?

74
00:03:11,751 --> 00:03:14,884
Did Cooper lose the bag during the jump?

75
00:03:14,884 --> 00:03:17,390
Was the money carried by water?

76
00:03:17,390 --> 00:03:19,270
Was it buried intentionally?

77
00:03:19,270 --> 00:03:22,089
Investigators could not establish a definitive explanation.

78
00:03:22,089 --> 00:03:24,501
The biggest question remains the simplest one:

79
00:03:24,501 --> 00:03:26,068
did Cooper survive?

80
00:03:26,068 --> 00:03:27,947
The conditions were brutal.

81
00:03:27,947 --> 00:03:29,514
It was dark.

82
00:03:29,514 --> 00:03:31,393
The weather was poor.

83
00:03:31,393 --> 00:03:34,526
Cooper was wearing business clothes and dress shoes.

84
00:03:34,526 --> 00:03:37,658
The parachute he used was not a modern steerable sport

85
00:03:37,658 --> 00:03:38,598
parachute.

86
00:03:38,598 --> 00:03:41,418
The terrain below was wooded and difficult.

87
00:03:41,418 --> 00:03:44,550
The FBI has long considered it possible that he died

88
00:03:44,550 --> 00:03:46,117
during the jump.

89
00:03:46,117 --> 00:03:48,623
But no body was ever found.

90
00:03:48,623 --> 00:03:50,816
No parachute was conclusively recovered.

91
00:03:50,816 --> 00:03:53,948
And despite decades of tips, no suspect was definitively identified

92
00:03:53,948 --> 00:03:56,455
as the man from Flight 305.

93
00:03:56,455 --> 00:03:59,180
One frequently discussed suspect was Richard Floyd McCoy,

94
00:03:59,180 --> 00:04:02,313
who carried out a similar hijacking and parachute escape less

95
00:04:02,313 --> 00:04:04,192
than five months later.

96
00:04:04,192 --> 00:04:07,231
The FBI investigated him, but eventually ruled him out,

97
00:04:07,231 --> 00:04:10,677
citing differences from the witness descriptions and other evidence.

98
00:04:10,677 --> 00:04:13,496
Even Cooper's famous name contains a mistake.

99
00:04:13,496 --> 00:04:16,002
The hijacker called himself Dan Cooper.

100
00:04:16,002 --> 00:04:17,569
The initials D.B.

101
00:04:17,569 --> 00:04:20,701
came from early press reporting after investigators questioned a different

102
00:04:20,701 --> 00:04:22,581
man with those initials.

103
00:04:22,581 --> 00:04:25,714
The mistake stuck, and the mystery became known around the

104
00:04:25,714 --> 00:04:27,593
world as the D.B.

105
00:04:27,593 --> 00:04:28,846
Cooper case.

106
00:04:28,846 --> 00:04:32,606
For forty-five years, the FBI continued reviewing tips and evidence.

107
00:04:32,606 --> 00:04:35,738
In July 2016, the Bureau announced that it was redirecting

108
00:04:35,738 --> 00:04:39,498
the investigative resources assigned to the case toward other priorities.

109
00:04:39,498 --> 00:04:42,630
The physical evidence was preserved, and the FBI said it

110
00:04:42,630 --> 00:04:45,763
would still consider specific new evidence connected to the parachutes

111
00:04:45,763 --> 00:04:47,329
or ransom money.

112
00:04:47,329 --> 00:04:50,775
That leaves us with an extraordinary set of facts.

113
00:04:50,775 --> 00:04:54,221
A man bought a ticket under a false name.

114
00:04:54,221 --> 00:04:56,727
He calmly hijacked a commercial aircraft.

115
00:04:56,727 --> 00:05:00,173
He collected two hundred thousand dollars and four parachutes.

116
00:05:00,173 --> 00:05:02,053
He released the passengers.

117
00:05:02,053 --> 00:05:04,152
Then, somewhere between Seattle and Reno,

118
00:05:04,152 --> 00:05:07,284
he lowered the rear stairs of a Boeing 727 and

119
00:05:07,284 --> 00:05:09,791
disappeared into a stormy November night.

120
00:05:09,791 --> 00:05:11,889
More than half a century later,

121
00:05:11,889 --> 00:05:14,928
we still do not know who he really was,

122
00:05:14,928 --> 00:05:18,061
whether he survived the jump, or what happened to most

123
00:05:18,061 --> 00:05:19,627
of the ransom.

124
00:05:19,627 --> 00:05:22,760
And that uncertainty may be the reason the story refuses

125
00:05:22,760 --> 00:05:24,013
to die.

126
00:05:24,013 --> 00:05:24,953
D.B.

127
00:05:24,953 --> 00:05:28,399
Cooper is not famous because investigators proved what happened.

128
00:05:28,399 --> 00:05:31,751
He is famous because after thousands of hours of investigation,

129
00:05:31,751 --> 00:05:34,163
hundreds of suspects, and decades of searching,

130
00:05:34,163 --> 00:05:36,669
the final scene is still missing.

131
00:05:36,669 --> 00:05:39,175
A man steps into the darkness.

132
00:05:39,175 --> 00:05:41,055
And never comes back.
